const PUBLIC_PRIVACY="<h1>Privacy Policy</h1><p class=\"muted\">Effective November 26, 2026</p><p>This notice covers Project Zero's public website, platform guides and customer account service. Public pages can be used without an account. Authorized accounts use Google sign-in. Public account registration, paid subscriptions and billing are not available.</p><h2>Public website and guides</h2><p>Hosting and security infrastructure may process network and request information to serve and protect the site.</p><h2>Optional analytics</h2><p>Analytics is disabled until you choose Allow analytics. SiteAPT, a related service under common ownership, measures public page paths, page titles, referrer domains, optional campaign tags, device and regional information, pseudonymous session identifiers, performance measurements, and named interaction events. It uses browser session storage for visitor/session identifiers and local storage to remember your choice. Calculator values, passwords and private project records are not sent by this website's analytics integration.</p><p>You can decline analytics or change your selection using Analytics preferences at the bottom of the public homepage or guides. Browser Do Not Track and Global Privacy Control are respected. Declining after allowing removes analytics session identifiers and reloads the page without the tracker. Previously received data is subject to SiteAPT's policies.</p><p><a href=\"https://siteapt.app/privacy\">SiteAPT privacy information</a></p><h2>External links and referrals</h2><p>Other websites have their own privacy policies. Some guides include clearly disclosed affiliate links. Ordinary official pricing and provider links are also available. If you choose an affiliate link, the external provider or affiliate platform may use cookies or attribution methods under its own policies. The private workspace is protected separately and is not included in public-site analytics.</p><h2>Customer accounts and sign-in</h2><p>We use Google sign-in to verify your identity and maintain your private workspace. We do not collect passwords or payment information.</p><p>With Google sign-in, we request only basic identity access (OpenID and email). We receive your Google account identifier and verified email address to authenticate you and match an authorized Project Zero account. We do not request access to Gmail, Google Drive, Calendar, contacts, or other Google content for sign-in.</p><p>Authorized workspaces may contain account details, workspace membership and roles, information you enter about projects, tasks and integrations, and security/session metadata. Our hosting provider may process IP addresses, request metadata and security logs to deliver and protect the service. The public account pages use no advertising or analytics cookies. When signing in, necessary secure cookies maintain the sign-in flow and session. A short-lived functional cookie remembers a recent visit to the private owner workspace so returning owners can reopen it; it grants no access and is not used for analytics.</p><h2>How we use information</h2><p>We use Google identity data only to authenticate you, manage access to your account, protect the service, and address security and support requests. We do not sell Google user data, use it for advertising or train AI models with it. Google access tokens are not retained by the sign-in service. Our use of Google user data is governed by the <a href=\"https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy\">Google API Services User Data Policy</a>, including its Limited Use requirements where applicable.</p><h2>Sharing and protection</h2><p>Google provides identity services; Cloudflare provides hosting, storage and security for Project Zero. Information may be processed by these service providers to deliver the requested functionality. We may disclose information where required by law or necessary to investigate misuse or protect rights and security. We do not disclose one customer workspace to another; authorized members of a workspace can access information according to their assigned roles.</p><h2>Retention and your choices</h2><p>We retain account and workspace information while needed to provide authorized access and meet legitimate security, support and legal obligations. Sign-in state expires after ten minutes; application sessions expire after twelve hours or can be revoked. You may revoke Google access in your <a href=\"https://myaccount.google.com/connections\">Google account settings</a>. Revoking Google access does not automatically remove information already stored in Project Zero. Account holders can export their project and task data from the account page and request account deletion by contacting privacy@projectzero.work. Deletion removes the active workspace, account and sessions; limited security counters and backup records may remain until their applicable retention periods expire. Expired sign-in challenges and sessions are cleaned up regularly. To request other access or correction, contact us; we will verify account ownership and review the request, subject to applicable retention obligations.</p><h2>Contact</h2><p>For privacy questions or data requests, email <a href=\"mailto:privacy@projectzero.work\">privacy@projectzero.work</a>. See also our <a href=\"/terms\">Terms of Service</a>.</p>";
const PUBLIC_TERMS="<h1>Terms of Service</h1><p class=\"muted\">Effective November 26, 2026</p><p>These terms govern use of the Project Zero public website, platform guides, account service. Project Zero helps business operators organize projects, development priorities, and operational information. Public account registration, paid subscriptions and live billing are not available.</p><h2>Account access</h2><p>Sign in using your authorized Google account. Access is limited to your own authorized workspace. You must have authority to act for a business or workspace you access, keep your account secure, and notify support if you suspect unauthorized access. Workspace permissions do not grant permission to access other workspaces or use third-party services beyond the authority granted by their owners.</p><h2>Acceptable use</h2><p>Use Project Zero lawfully. Do not misuse accounts, circumvent security or access controls, submit malicious content, interfere with service operations, or upload data you do not have authority to process. You retain responsibility for information you provide and decisions you make using the service.</p><h2>Business information and third parties</h2><p>Project Zero is an organizational and reporting tool, not professional financial, legal or tax advice. Verify important information against its source before acting. The platform guides and calculators provide informational comparisons and estimates; verify current provider conditions and costs directly. Some guide links are disclosed affiliate referrals. Third-party integrations and linked services have their own terms, privacy notices and availability. Project Zero does not authorize charges, purchases, public deployments or other external commitments merely because a task or recommendation is recorded.</p><h2>Availability and future paid services</h2><p>Project Zero is evolving software. Features, capacity, and availability may change. Keep copies of important information and do not store sensitive customer data, passwords or credentials. No credit card is required, and no paid subscription, automatic renewal or charge is initiated by using an account. Any future paid offering will present its price, billing conditions and cancellation terms and require your explicit decision before purchase. We may suspend access to address misuse, security or legal obligations.</p><h2>Privacy and changes</h2><p>Our <a href=\"/privacy\">Privacy Policy</a> explains how account information is handled. We will update these terms when the service changes and make the current version available here. Nothing in these terms limits rights that cannot be waived under applicable law.</p><h2>Contact</h2><p>For questions about these terms, email <a href=\"mailto:legal@projectzero.work\">legal@projectzero.work</a>. For assistance email <a href=\"mailto:support@projectzero.work\">support@projectzero.work</a>.</p>";
function publicLegal(req,path){
 const isPrivacy=path==='/privacy',title=isPrivacy?'Privacy Policy':'Terms of Service';
 const content=isPrivacy?PUBLIC_PRIVACY:PUBLIC_TERMS;
 const markup='<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>'+title+' | Project Zero</title><meta name="description" content="Project Zero '+title+'"><link rel="canonical" href="'+BASE+path+'"><style>'+CSS+'</style></head><body><header><nav class="wrap"><a class="brand" href="/?home=1"><img src="/logo.svg" alt="">Project Zero</a><a href="/?home=1">Home</a></nav></header><main class="wrap" style="padding-top:40px;padding-bottom:48px"><article class="detail" style="max-width:850px;margin:auto">'+content+'</article></main><footer class="wrap" style="padding:24px 28px;display:flex;gap:20px;flex-wrap:wrap"><a href="/?home=1">Home</a><a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a><a href="/terms">Terms of Service</a><a href="mailto:support@projectzero.work">Support</a></footer></body></html>';
 return response(req,markup,'text/html; charset=utf-8');
}
// The guide Worker owns the live catalog; a service binding keeps the main sitemap complete.
async function publicSitemap(req,env){
 const header='<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?><urlset xmlns="http://www.sitemaps.org/schemas/sitemap/0.9">';
 const main=['/','/demo','/privacy','/terms'].map(p=>'<url><loc>'+BASE+p+'</loc></url>').join('');
 try{
  if(!env?.GUIDE_SITEMAP)throw Error('Guide sitemap binding unavailable');
  const r=await env.GUIDE_SITEMAP.fetch(new Request('https://guides.projectzero.work/sitemap.xml'));
  if(!r.ok||!/^application\/xml\b/i.test(r.headers.get('content-type')||''))throw Error('Guide sitemap unavailable');
  const xml=await r.text();
  if(xml.length>500000||!xml.startsWith(header)||!xml.endsWith('</urlset>'))throw Error('Invalid guide sitemap');
  const entries=xml.slice(header.length,-9);
  const rx=/<url><loc>(https:\/\/guides\.projectzero\.work\/(?:[a-z0-9-]+(?:\/[a-z0-9-]+)?)?)<\/loc>(?:<lastmod>\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}<\/lastmod>)?<\/url>/g;
  const matches=[...entries.matchAll(rx)],urls=matches.map(m=>m[1]);
  if(!urls.length||matches.map(m=>m[0]).join('')!==entries||new Set(urls).size!==urls.length||!urls.includes('https://guides.projectzero.work/')||!urls.includes('https://guides.projectzero.work/privacy'))throw Error('Invalid guide sitemap entries');
  if(urls.some(v=>!/^https:\/\/guides\.projectzero\.work\/(?:|privacy|platforms|compare|methodology|tools|setups|(?:platforms|categories|compare|tools|setups)\/[a-z0-9-]+)$/.test(v)))throw Error('Unexpected guide route');
  return response(req,header+main+entries+'</urlset>','application/xml; charset=utf-8',200,'public, max-age=300, s-maxage=300');
 }catch{return response(req,'Sitemap temporarily unavailable. Please retry.','text/plain; charset=utf-8',503,'no-store')}
}
